@page "/guides/how-to-run-a-badminton-round-robin"
@using PegboardWebSite.Services
@{
    Layout = "_LayoutMarketing";
    ViewData["Title"] = "How to run a badminton round robin: calculate the schedule before you promise the finish time";
    ViewData["Description"] = "A practical badminton round-robin guide: match-count maths, circle-method draws, court capacity, byes, fixed pairs versus rotating doubles, tie-breaks, time limits and what to do when a player withdraws.";
    ViewData["OgTitle"] = "How to run a badminton round robin properly";

    const string published = "2026-07-05";   // first publication - held deliberately; GuideSchema
                                             // ties dateModified to it (#396), so advancing it
                                             // would claim this page is new. Revised 2026-08-26.
    const string canonical = "https://www.epegboard.com/guides/how-to-run-a-badminton-round-robin";

    var faqPairs = new (string Q, string A)[]
    {
        ("How many matches are in a badminton round robin?",
         "With N entrants, each playing every other entrant once, there are N(N−1)/2 matches. Six pairs therefore create 15 matches; eight create 28; twelve create 66. This is why field size matters more than it first appears."),
        ("How many rounds does a round robin need?",
         "With an even number of entrants and enough courts, the circle method produces N−1 logical rounds. With an odd number, add a bye and there are N rounds, with one entrant resting each round. If you have fewer courts than matches in a logical round, those rounds must be split into additional court waves."),
        ("Can doubles partners rotate in a round robin?",
         "Yes, but that is a different scheduling problem from a fixed-pair round robin. If individuals rotate partners, you need to define what 'round robin' means: equal games, every possible partner once, balanced opponents, or an American-style individual competition. Those goals are not identical."),
        ("What tie-break should a round robin use?",
         "There is no universal tie-break for an informal club event. Publish the sequence before play - for example wins, then head-to-head for a two-way tie, then points difference. Also define how multi-way ties and withdrawals are handled so you do not invent a rule after seeing who benefits."),
        ("What happens if someone withdraws halfway through?",
         "Decide in advance. Common options are to retain completed matches or expunge that entrant's results so everyone is compared across the same opponent set. The latter is cleaner statistically but can erase real matches. The right choice depends on the event; the wrong choice is making it after seeing the table.")
    };

    var articleJsonLd = GuideSchema.Article(
        "How to run a badminton round robin: calculate the schedule before you promise the finish time",
        "A practical badminton round-robin guide: match-count maths, circle-method draws, court capacity, byes, fixed pairs versus rotating doubles, tie-breaks, time limits and what to do when a player withdraws.",
        canonical, published);
    var faqJsonLd = GuideSchema.Faq(faqPairs);
}

@section Head {
    <script type="application/ld+json">@Html.Raw(articleJsonLd)</script>
    <script type="application/ld+json">@Html.Raw(faqJsonLd)</script>
}

